Replace the curl-based exit-IP health check with a stdlib SOCKS5 probe.

check_proxy_health() shelled out to `curl -s -w "\n%{time_total} %{http_code}" -x
socks5h://...` to learn the exit IP and the latency, which made the image carry
curl plus libcurl, brotli, c-ares, libidn2, libpsl, libunistring and nghttp2
(~4.8 MB of libraries) for a single call site.

_probe_exit_ip() now speaks SOCKS5 directly on a socket (RFC 1928 CONNECT with a
domain name, RFC 1929 username/password when LOCAL_PROXY_USER/PASS are set) and
sends a plain HTTP/1.1 GET, decoding both Content-Length and chunked bodies.
Behaviour is unchanged: for each proxy host (including the "::" ->
[::1]/127.0.0.1 fallback list) it tries http://ip.sb and then
http://api.ipify.org, returns {"ok": True, "ip": ..., "latency_ms": ...} on HTTP
200 with a non-empty body, and returns None on any failure so the caller keeps
running its existing port/diagnostic fallback.

The request keeps curl's default User-Agent on purpose: ip.sb answers 301 to
https for unknown agents (verified: no-UA, curl/*, wget/* -> 200, a custom agent
-> 301), so a bespoke header would silently turn the probe into a permanent
failure. The timer starts before the TCP connect, like curl's %{time_total},
so the reported latency stays comparable (measured delta vs curl: ~14 ms).

